  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nUpper ridge continues to dominate the southeastern US with one \ntrough noted just southeast of the Hudson Bay and a deeper upper low \noff the Pacific Northwest coast. This keeps weaker southwest flow \naloft in our area with the stronger upper flow towards the \nmountainous regions. At the surface, low pressure has developed in \nthe Dakotas with its trough axis into central NE and western KS, all \nin response to that troughing pattern. This has resulted in somewhat \nbreezy southerly winds across eastern KS. The deeper moisture \nresides in TX/LA within remnants of the tropical system, and you \nhave to get to OK before finding dew points in the 70s, but we are \ngetting close in our area. The southerly low-level flow should \ngradually pull some of this moisture north and help to offset some \nof the deeper mixing that has occurred in recent days. Heat indices \nso far today have reached the 99-104 degree range and there is still \ntime to heat up a little more this afternoon.\n\nThe overall sentiment was to not make the headline decision overly \ncomplicated with this forecast cycle. The upper pattern shows an \nomega block developing into the weekend with the ridge retrograding \nand becoming centered right over the Central Plains. This is not \nconducive to conditions changing anytime soon, and ensemble cluster \nanalysis continues to show upper ridging holding over the central US \nthrough at least Tuesday, possibly Wednesday. Forecast temperature \nranges also remain small through Tuesday with highs in the upper 90s \nto around 100 and lows in the mid to upper 70s. These ranges only \nwiden slightly on Wednesday. It isn't until Thursday that longer \nrange guidance starts to show some divergence in the model \nsolutions, depending on how well low pressure systems riding the \nridge will actually weaken it. This means there is some potential \nfor lowering temperatures closer to 90, but even that is not certain \nas some solutions continue to keep the ridge overhead, which would \nlead to continued heat. Based on a combination of the pattern being \nfavorable for prolonged heat, high confidence in hot temperatures, \nwarm lows providing little opportunity for relief, and increased \nlikelihood for impacts with the holiday weekend and outdoor \nactivities, we felt it was prudent to upgrade the advisory to an \nExtreme Heat Warning and extend it through Tuesday. Cannot rule out \npossibly extending it into Wednesday as well. It should also be \nmentioned that even if dew points end up being slightly lower than \nforecast and heat indices don't reach or exceed 105 degrees in many \nplaces, 100-104 degree heat indices for multiple days in a row \naren't going to make much difference from an impacts standpoint, \nespecially with warm mornings compounding the heat stress each day. \n\nIn addition to late next week having lower confidence in seeing \nmeaningful relief from the heat, there are some low chances for \nrain. But the same model differences leading to that uncertainty in \ntemperatures also make it hard to have much confidence in rain \nchances, as the track of the systems (if they hold) favor rain \nchances outside of our area.",
